  "text": "Medb, also written Maeve, Medbh, Medhbh, and Meave, was an Irish goddess and heroine. From her royal seat at Cruachan, the capital of western Connacht, Queen Medb ruled with power and vigor. Behind her stood the figure of the goddess of the land. The king's marriage to her granted sovereignty over the territory and bound him with sacred vows, the geis. As a goddess, Medb is linked with the five fierce war figures known together as the Badb, sometimes including the Mórrígan. Her capital is now Rathcrogan near Tulsk in County Roscommon. More than seventy ritual and royal sites of the Celtic Iron Age remain there. Medb was born in a nearby cave named Oweynagat, of the obscure woman Crochan Crocderg, the blood-red cup. Crochan had fled there with her mistress, the reborn fairy queen Étain. Medb's name means mead or intoxication. In legend she was fiery and self-willed. She kept Ailill mac Máta as her consort and took Fergus mac Róich as her favored lover. Her cattle raid on Ulster begins with a pillow-talk scene. Ailill claimed to own more than Medb, a claim that lowered her standing under Brehon law. When Ailill showed a white bull she could not match, Medb sought its equal. Only the brown Donn Cuailnge of Ulster matched it. Medb planned to seize it, knowing the curse of the goddess Macha would leave the Ulstermen unable to fight. The hero Cúchulainn alone held the pass against her champions. Medb won the bull, but the two bulls killed each other in a great battle. Medb met her death on the island of Clothrann in Lough Ree. Her nephew Furbaide Ferbend struck her down, for she had killed his mother, Clothra. She is said to be buried in the great tumulus of Knocknarea above Sligo. Under the name Medb Lethderg, she married the kings of Tara, confirming her role as the great goddess of sovereignty.",
